{"result": {"response": {"id": "resp_03a8d688484cb75d0169bb3ca702188191b75058772d6120e0", "created": 1773878439, "model": "gpt-5.2-2025-12-11", "object": "chat.completion", "system_fingerprint": null, "choices": [{"finish_reason": "stop", "index": 0, "message": {"content": "{\"title\":\"Physical Exam, Lymph Node Assessment, and Groin/Underarm Findings\",\"summary\":\"This community centers on clinician-led physical examinations and targeted assessments for lumps, lymph node enlargement, and related symptoms in key anatomical regions (groin/inguinal area, underarm/armpit, and pelvis). The network links general examination activities (vital signs and pain assessment) with more specialized follow-ups after a confirmed cutaneous squamous cell carcinoma (CSCC) diagnosis—specifically head-to-toe skin exams and lymph node exams to evaluate potential spread. It also includes a breast-exam subcluster where clinical breast exam (CBE) involves palpation of the breast and armpit, sometimes with a nurse/assistant present in an exam room, and it notes imaging-related positioning of an ultrasound probe near the armpit. Overall, the relationships describe a clinical workflow for detecting and surveilling abnormalities (lumps, swollen nodes, dull groin/pelvic pain) and, when cancer involvement is known/suspected, escalation to surgical management such as groin dissection (inguinal lymph node removal).\",\"findings\":[{\"summary\":\"Physical examination is the organizing activity tying together symptom reporting, palpation targets, and clinician workflow\",\"explanation\":\"The core operational node is “performing a physical exam,” explicitly framed as the activity constituting the physical exam used for evaluation and planning. This exam is described as including components such as checking vital signs and assessing pain response to touch, indicating a structured approach that mixes general health assessment with targeted evaluation of complaint areas. [Data: Entities (5647, 5685, 5686); Relationships (12674, 12675, 12676)]\\n\\nA hematologist/oncologist is specifically connected as the clinician who performs this physical exam, signaling that the workflow is relevant to oncologic/hematologic contexts where lymph nodes, spleen, and liver may be assessed. This positions the community as a clinical decision-support cluster rather than a social or organizational network. [Data: Entities (5644, 5647); Relationships (12649)]\"},{\"summary\":\"Groin anatomy and inguinal lymph nodes form a key surveillance and management pathway\",\"explanation\":\"The groin is defined as an anatomical region with many lymph nodes (inguinal lymph nodes) and is repeatedly referenced as a primary site for palpation and evaluation, particularly in oncology-related contexts where lymphatic spread or recurrence may occur. The relationships explicitly ground that the inguinal lymph nodes are located in the groin, anchoring subsequent diagnostic and treatment steps to this region. [Data: Entities (223, 6047, 6240); Relationships (13824, 14359)]\\n\\nThis cluster extends beyond examination into intervention: “groin dissection” is described as removal of groin/inguinal lymph nodes when nodes are known or suspected to contain cancer, including recurrence scenarios and contexts where prior radiation therapy might influence management decisions. The relationship directly ties groin dissection to inguinal lymph nodes, reflecting an escalation path from detection/surveillance to surgery. [Data: Entities (6178, 6047); Relationships (14174)]\"},{\"summary\":\"Underarm/armpit lymph node checks are emphasized as standard exam targets and overlap with breast evaluation\",\"explanation\":\"The underarm (axilla) and armpit are both described as key anatomical sites routinely checked for lymph node enlargement during physical examinations. A specific relationship states that lymph nodes are checked for enlargement in the underarm during the physical exam, reinforcing this as a standard node-assessment location alongside other regions. [Data: Entities (222, 1307); Relationships (12075)]\\n\\nThe armpit is also explicitly included in clinical breast examinations: the CBE is an exam of the bare breast performed to check for lumps or changes and includes palpation of the armpit region. This creates a bridge between general lymphadenopathy surveillance and breast-focused screening/diagnostics. [Data: Entities (3708, 271, 1307); Relationships (7643)]\"},{\"summary\":\"Clinical breast exam (CBE) is a defined sub-workflow with setting and staffing elements\",\"explanation\":\"CBE is characterized as a provider-performed exam of the bare breast to check for lumps or changes, and it may be conducted seated and/or lying down while palpating the breast and armpit. This situates “lumps” as a key exam finding that motivates careful palpation in both breast tissue and adjacent lymphatic drainage areas. [Data: Entities (3708, 271, 1307); Relationships (7643)]\\n\\nOperationally, the CBE is described as occurring in an exam room and potentially involving a nurse or assistant present to support the process. These relationships define the immediate clinical environment and roles involved, reflecting standard care workflows rather than ad hoc assessment. [Data: Entities (3700, 3701, 3708); Relationships (7611, 7614)]\"},{\"summary\":\"Lumps and patient reporting are framed as actionable clinical signals tied to lymph node evaluation\",\"explanation\":\"Lumps are described as palpable findings—raised or abnormal areas under the skin—that clinicians look for during physical examinations (including breast exams) and that patients are commonly instructed to monitor and report. This frames lumps as a trigger for clinical attention and downstream evaluation. [Data: Entities (271, 3708); Relationships (255, 7643)]\\n\\nA relationship explicitly states that the patient is told to report if they have felt any lumps, and it connects this reporting to exam findings including lymph node checks. This indicates an information flow from patient self-observation to clinician assessment, with potential implications for timely detection of abnormal changes. [Data: Relationships (255); Entities (271, 437, 5647)]\"},{\"summary\":\"Post-CSCC diagnosis follow-up includes both skin-wide evaluation and targeted lymph node assessment for spread\",\"explanation\":\"A confirmed CSCC diagnosis is linked to two explicit follow-up steps: a head-to-toe skin exam to look for other lesions and a lymph node exam to assess potential spread. This defines a surveillance bundle after diagnosis that addresses both local/field disease (skin exam) and regional dissemination risk (nodal exam). [Data: Entities (440, 436, 437); Relationships (603, 604)]\\n\\nThe lymph node exam is described as palpation of key lymph node regions—particularly the groin, as well as the neck and underarm/axillary areas—to detect swelling, firmness, or other abnormalities suggestive of involvement. This aligns anatomically with the broader community emphasis on groin/inguinal nodes and underarm nodes as high-yield assessment sites. [Data: Entities (437, 223, 222); Relationships (604, 12075, 13824)]\"},{\"summary\":\"Dull groin/pelvic pain is represented as a symptom node connected to specific anatomical framing\",\"explanation\":\"Two symptom entities—“dull pain in groin or pelvis” and “dull groin or pelvic pain”—are both grounded as persistent, low-grade pain localized to the groin and/or pelvis. The relationships explicitly link the pelvis to each symptom concept, and also link the groin to at least one of them, reinforcing the symptom’s anatomical definition rather than an inferred diagnosis. [Data: Entities (3173, 3408); Relationships (6238, 6739, 6275)]\\n\\nThe groin entity description further contextualizes that dull pain may be felt in the groin or pelvis and is listed as a symptom to report in prostate-related conditions, but the dataset does not provide additional relationships to specific prostate diagnoses beyond that contextual note. Accordingly, the community captures the symptom-location mapping without attributing causality. [Data: Entities (223, 3173, 3408); Relationships (6275, 6739)]\"},{\"summary\":\"Imaging adjacency is limited but present: armpit as a placement region for ultrasound probe\",\"explanation\":\"Beyond palpation-based exams, the armpit is also connected to imaging workflow via a relationship stating that an ultrasound probe (transducer) may be placed below the armpit during ultrasound imaging. This suggests that the axillary region can be used as an access/positioning site for evaluating structures near the breast and adjacent tissues. [Data: Entities (1307); Relationships (7759)]\\n\\nWithin the overall community, this imaging linkage complements the CBE and lymph node assessment emphasis in the armpit/underarm area, but it remains a single, narrowly specified connection rather than a full diagnostic imaging pathway. [Data: Entities (1307, 3708); Relationships (7759, 7643)]\"}],\"rating\":4.2,\"rating_explanation\":\"Impact is moderate because the community describes clinically important cancer surveillance and escalation pathways (CSCC follow-up, lymph node assessment, and potential groin node surgery) but contains no evidence of malicious activity or broader societal harm beyond patient-care significance.\"}", "role": "assistant", "tool_calls": null, "function_call": null}, "provider_specific_fields": {"native_finish_reason": "stop"}}], "usage": {"completion_tokens": 1866, "prompt_tokens": 4412, "total_tokens": 6278, "completion_tokens_details": {"accepted_prediction_tokens": null, "audio_tokens": null, "reasoning_tokens": 0, "rejected_prediction_tokens": null, "text_tokens": null}, "prompt_tokens_details": {"audio_tokens": null, "cached_tokens": 0, "text_tokens": null, "image_tokens": null}}, "service_tier": null}}}